Generate a complete, self-contained Terraform repository for a private S3 bucket served through CloudFront using Origin Access Control (OAC) — no public bucket ACLs, no legacy Origin Access Identity.

SPECS:
  output directory: {workspace}
  project/environment prefix: {project_name}
  AWS region: {aws_region}
  bucket name: {bucket_name}
  custom domain: {domain_name}
  versioning: {enable_versioning}

Emit these files, each as its own path-bearing fence rooted at {workspace}/:
  versions.tf       — terraform block, required_providers (hashicorp/aws, pinned major version), required_version
  variables.tf       — one variable per SPEC above, with type and description, no hardcoded defaults for anything SPEC-supplied
  main.tf            — aws_s3_bucket (private), aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block (block everything), aws_s3_bucket_versioning if requested, aws_cloudfront_origin_access_control, aws_cloudfront_distribution (OAC origin, viewer_certificate using ACM cert only if {domain_name} is not NONE, otherwise default CloudFront certificate), aws_s3_bucket_policy restricting GetObject to the CloudFront distribution's OAC via aws:SourceArn condition. If {domain_name} is not NONE, also emit aws_acm_certificate (us-east-1, DNS validation), aws_route53_record for validation and for the alias — assume the domain's hosted zone already exists and look it up with a data "aws_route53_zone" block by name, do not create the zone.
  outputs.tf         — bucket name, bucket arn, distribution id, distribution domain name, and the custom domain if set
  terraform.tfvars.example — one line per variable with a realistic placeholder value, no real secrets
  README.md          — what this deploys, prerequisites (AWS credentials, existing Route53 zone if a domain is used), and the exact terraform init/plan/apply commands to run from {workspace}

Every resource name must incorporate {project_name} so this repo is safely deployable alongside others in the same account. Do not invent variables not listed in SPECS without also adding them to variables.tf with a sensible default. Do not include a provider "aws" block with a hardcoded profile or access key — region only, sourced from the aws_region variable.

After the files, include ONE pathless bash fence that does exactly:
cd {workspace} && terraform init -backend=false && terraform validate
No other commentary outside the fences.
